{"type":"text","timestamp":1786048765317,"sessionID":"ses_02733c06fffeyj3WksNPKgiMHi","part":{"id":"prt_fd8cd5640001MOJnHnMdOcBt8j","messageID":"msg_fd8cd42d20014An1iS7LrsaIeX","sessionID":"ses_02733c06fffeyj3WksNPKgiMHi","type":"text","text":"I have formulated a refined cognitive hypothesis based on `evidence_accumulation_per_run` and implemented it as a PyMC model.\n\nHere is a summary of the hypothesis and how it was implemented:\n- **Hypothesis:** Random-looking sequences are judged by an evidence-accumulation process where each distinct run provides a baseline quantum of evidence for randomness, but this total evidence is exponentially discounted by the sequence's absolute deviation from an inferred messy prototype (ideal balance and alternation rates). This reflects a sharp psychological generalization gradient where perceived randomness drops off multiplicatively as sequences violate prototype expectations.\n- **Model Name:** `exponential_discount_per_run`\n- **Implementation:** The model uses the precomputed `alts_a`, `p_a`, and `p_alts_a` columns. The model calculates the exponential decay based on absolute distance (via `pt.abs` and `pt.exp`) instead of standardizing into separate squared components, applying it as a multiplicative discount to the base evidence (`runs`).\n\nThe files have been generated at the requested paths and pass all validation checks.","time":{"start":1786048763457,"end":1786048765302}}}
